The site is the node: G2 is decided, and the reason is Jekyll's

Escalation a31b asked two questions of the node-study seat. This answers the second.

## Decided

Her words, 2026-09-16:

> i do think the site is the node because itll build with its resources handy in jekyll
> data. The -node paradigm gives us site clusters that share a build root context

**This repository is the node.** Not a sibling holding the site as a library holding.

The argument is Jekyll's, and it only runs one way. A node's holdings sit under its own
root — so if the site *is* the node, every holding is inside the build root and reachable
from `_data`. The library becomes readable by the site that keeps it, for free, with no
copying step and nothing to keep in sync.

Invert it and the site is a holding *inside* the node. Its build root is then a
subdirectory that cannot see its siblings, and every shared resource needs a mechanism
somebody has to build and maintain.

The unit that buys is the one she named: a **site cluster sharing one build root context**.
That is also what turns member-site provisioning — G4 — into the library admitting a
holding, rather than a deployment problem.

## What changed in the file

G2 was *"a recommendation on where the node lives, written so a person can decide it in
one sitting."* The person decided, so the goal advances rather than closing: write down
the **consequences** — what moves to the root, what `_data` may and may not reach into,
and what a second site in the cluster would need.

The decision and its reasoning are kept as a comment on the goal rather than deleted with
it. A later reader needs to know this was *chosen*, and on what grounds — not merely that
it is so. That is the seat's whole premise, and the escalation exists because two such
choices had already gone unwritten.

Mounting anything is still out of scope and still its own pull request by whoever owns it.

## Verified

`advocate.yml` parses; the node seat still carries G1–G4.

## Still open — the other half of a31b

**G4 needs your paragraph.** You said you made choices after talking with board members,
and want to use them to imagine what public media offers under the library's categories.
They are not written anywhere an agent can find. The seat runs without them; it just
cannot become concrete. a31b stays open for that alone, and I have narrowed it to say so.

